#*: {{quote|ang|''Þā behēoldon swīðe ġeorne þā ċeapmenn hine, and be him on ġeþance smēadon '''hwæt''' manna hē bēon sċolde.''|The businessmen looked at him very closely and tried to figure out '''what kind''' of person he might be.}}
# [[how many]] or [[how much]] ''(+genitive)''
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=Cd4UAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA242 “Palm Sunday: On the Lord’s Passion”]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Iūdās sōna ēode tō þāra Iudēisċena rǣde and openlīċe befræġn '''hwæt''' hīe him fēos ġeūðen ġif hē þone Hǣlend him belǣwan meahte.''|Judas immediately went to the Jewish council and openly asked '''how much''' money they would give him for betraying Jesus.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=Bs1nAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA57 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius’]] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Be þām hringum man meahte witan '''hwæt''' Rōmāna duguþe ġefeallen wæs, for þon þe hit wæs þēaw mid him on þām dagum þæt nān ōðer ne mōste gyldenne hring werian būtan hē æðeles cynnes wǣre.''|You could tell by the rings '''how much''' of the Roman nobility had fallen, because the custom back then was that no one could wear a gold ring unless they were from a noble family.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=LKe0AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA220 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius']] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Hwæðer Rōmāne hit witon nū ǣnegum menn tō seċġenne '''hwæt''' heora folces on Ispāniam on fēawum ġēarum forwurde?''|Do the Romans know any more '''how many''' of their people perished in Spain within a few years?}}
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=trkLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA204 The Dialogues of Solomon and Saturn]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Sæġe mē '''hwæt''' fisċcynna sīe.''|Tell me '''how many''' species of fish there are.}}
# [[anything]], [[something]]
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=xBNXAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA71 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius']] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Sēo lēo bringþ hungrigum hwelpum '''hwæt''' tō etenne.''|The lion brings hungry cubs '''something''' to eat.}}
